# Line endings are pinned to LF, and this file is the only thing that decides.
#
# The machine this was written on has core.autocrlf=true, so without this file
# a checkout rewrites the working tree to CRLF. Two tracked files cannot
# survive that:
#
#   bin/tracedoc          a #!/bin/sh script Claude Code runs through sh on
#                         every hook, where a trailing \r turns each line into
#                         a command that does not exist
#   cmd/tracedoc/main.go  usage() is a raw string literal, so its bytes are the
#                         program's bytes; a CRLF checkout of the source would
#                         stop matching the committed binary and fail the
#                         staleness guard for a reason that has nothing to do
#                         with the source
#
# The explicit eol skips git's binary detection, so the shipped builds are
# marked -text by hand below. Without that, a checkout would rewrite every byte
# of them.
* text=auto eol=lf

bin/tracedoc-darwin-amd64 -text
bin/tracedoc-darwin-arm64 -text
bin/tracedoc-linux-amd64 -text
bin/tracedoc-linux-arm64 -text
bin/tracedoc-windows-amd64.exe -text
bin/tracedoc-windows-arm64.exe -text
